  "problem": "At time $t=5\\,\\text{s}$ the eagle is located at a point on $C$ with $y$-coordinate equal to $\\frac{95}{3}\\,\\text{m}.$ Use the tangent line approximation to approximate the $y$-coordinate of the eagle's location when $t=\\frac{110}{23}\\,\\text{s}.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $y(\\frac{110}{23})≈$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\,\\text{m}$",
  "worked_solution": "The tangent line approximation to $y(t)$ at the point $a=5$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(t) & =y(a)+{y}^{′}(a)(t-a) \\\\  & =y(5)+{y}^{′}(5)(t-5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $y(5)=\\frac{95}{3},$ and since ${y}^{′}(t)={t}^{2}-2,$ then ${y}^{′}(5)=25-2=23.$ \n\nSo, the tangent line approximation is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(t) & =\\frac{95}{3}+23(t-5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can approximate $y(\\frac{110}{23})$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=\\frac{110}{23}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(\\frac{110}{23}) & ≈L(\\frac{110}{23}) \\\\  & =\\frac{95}{3}+23(\\frac{110}{23}-5) \\\\  & =\\frac{95}{3}+23(-\\frac{5}{23}) \\\\  & =\\frac{95}{3}-5 \\\\  & =\\frac{80}{3}\\,\\text{m}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
